# TradingView BOS Forex Swing Break

## Source
- Source: [[sources/tradingview-mechanical-strategy-scripts]]
- Page / Timestamp: TradingView script `Break of structure (BOS) forex Strategy`, author handle `TJalam`, invite-only strategy page, published 2021-04-01 and updated 2023-05-06, accessed 2026-05-22, https://www.tradingview.com/script/HKcm1dwo-Break-of-structure-BOS-forex-Strategy/

## Mechanics

### Entry
Use M15/H1 FX baseline.

- Define the active time window for the currency pair and session being tested.
- Detect the most recent previous swing high and swing low on the fixed timeframe.
- Long setup:
  - Price breaks the previous swing high.
  - Break occurs inside the configured long-permitted session window.
  - Enter long after the break-of-structure confirmation close.
- Short setup:
  - Price breaks the previous swing low.
  - Break occurs inside the configured short-permitted session window.
  - Enter short after the break-of-structure confirmation close.
- Enter only with no existing position.

### Exit
- Source public text is thin on exits; V5 baseline supplies deterministic safety exits:
  - Stop at the opposite recent swing plus ATR buffer.
  - Target at fixed R multiple.
  - Optional opposite-BOS signal-reversal exit.
  - Session-end flat.

### Stop Loss
- Long: stop below the latest valid swing low or ATR(14) * 1.5, whichever is wider.
- Short: stop above the latest valid swing high or ATR(14) * 1.5, whichever is wider.

### Position Sizing
V5 default: fixed-risk $1,000 for P2 baseline, one position per symbol/magic.

### Zusatzliche Filter
- Session window is mandatory because source explicitly ties direction to currency/time-zone behavior.
- Confirmation close vs intrabar break is a P3 axis.

## Concepts (what kind of strategy is this)
- [[concepts/break-of-structure]] - entry follows break of previous swing structure.
- [[concepts/forex-session-bias]] - source restricts long/short timing by pair and time zone.
- [[concepts/swing-breakout]] - previous high/low swing is the actionable level.

